{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he who sprinkles the water of impurity shall wash his garments.</b> Since he is pure and purifies the impure, why should he become impure such that he requires washing his garments? But it is because of the splashing of the water that rebounds upon the one who sprinkles, that he contracts impurity from it, and therefore he washes his garments — thus I have heard. But the view of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Gemara of Yoma is that the one who sprinkles does not require washing his garments, and so they said: What is 'the one who sprinkles' (מזה)? A carrier (נושא). And the reason Scripture elevates the one who sprinkles to the language of 'carrier' is because the carrier of the waters of impurity is not required to wash his garments except for the measure of sprinkling, which is enough to dip the tips of the hyssop stalks."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites Yoma 44a-b; the phrase ראשי גבעולין refers to the tips of the hyssop stalks used in the sprinkling rite (cf. Numbers 19:18)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:02.510368274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}